# v0.25.11 — on-chain messaging (votes, moderation, DMs, agent SDK), bridge ledger/ops hardening, authority monitoring, website status pages

**Baseline**: `v0.25.10` tagged at `6fc1ed373b` (2026-10-07). Sections below cover `v0.25.10..51c485eb4` — 551 commits accrued on `main` after the tag, per the release-tag policy (`AGENTS.md`: the version's changelog is finalised in the tagged commit; later entries go to the next version's log).
**Date**: 2026-10-16.
**Tag**: `v0.25.11`.

### Monitoring: authority watch list derived from live chain parameters

The authority-balance exporter used to read `AITBC_WATCH_ACCOUNTS` — a static env list the unit's own comment said to "edit when an authority is rotated". After the KR-2/KR-3 rotations it watched the *retired* addresses: `AuthorityFloatCritical` fired for hours on the zeroed old settlement account while the live authorities — and both governance executors — went unwatched.

- `f5dda3594e` — `authority-balances-textfile.py` now reads `GET <RPC>/rpc/state/snapshot` every minutely run and resolves `escrow_settlement` and `bridge_release` from the live `chain_parameters`, so a rotation is picked up at the next tick with no unit edit. `AITBC_WATCH_ACCOUNTS` becomes optional fallback + extras; a role that falls back still reports `scrape_success` 0 so `AuthorityBalanceUnreadable` fires instead of silently trusting a stale address. A missing account row (HTTP 404) reads as balance 0 / nonce 0 / success 1 — an unfunded authority is *readable*, not an error. The unit's env line now carries the current authorities as the fallback. `tests/unit/test_authority_balances_textfile.py` rewritten for the new behaviour (snapshot-derived addresses, fallback, rotation between runs).
- `026356a029` — the `governance_executors` chain parameter becomes a list-valued role: every member gets its own `governance_executor` series (a malformed member marks the role stale without dropping the valid ones). New hub alerts `GovernanceExecutorFloatLow` (< 2,160,000 units — six `GOVERNANCE_EXECUTE` fees) and `GovernanceExecutorFloatCritical` (< 720,000 — two fees), exclusive bands matching the settlement pair.
- `be3125ad62` — `bond_slash` joins the watched roles. The earlier commit left it out on the mistaken assumption that BOND_SLASH pays no fee; it does (360,000 units per slash), so the unfunded signer was a live blind spot. `PARAMETER_ROLES` gains `bond_slash` ← `bond_slash_authority` (parameter-derived, auto-follows rotation) plus `BondSlashAuthorityFloatLow` (< 3,600,000 — ten fees) and `BondSlashAuthorityFloatCritical` (< 720,000 — two fees), exclusive bands.
- `88db185daa` — three `AITBC_WATCH_ACCOUNTS` extras close the non-parameter blind spots: `governance_proposer` (`0xD8168A8b…`, fee-paying service identity, float bands 3,600,000 / 720,000), `validator_hub` (`0x2aD555A9…`, 36M parked float — a `ValidatorHubAccountMoved` moved-watch at critical severity; validators are never debited, so movement itself is the anomaly), and `proposer_retired` (`0x02B8F2C6…`, ~36T residue — same moved-watch at warning severity; legacy genesis/admin roles mean movement can still be legitimate). The unit comment records that extras do not auto-follow a rotation.
- `d70099eeaf` (variant head, unpushed) — lowers `GovernanceProposerFloatLow` to < 1,800,000 (below five fees) so up to four submissions pass before the warning; the pushed head `88db185daa` warns on the first. Exactly one of the two heads is intended for push; the other stays local.
- **Live result (2026-10-07)**: hub deploy at `88db185daa` — the exporter reports every managed role plus the three extras with `scrape_success` 1 and all float/moved alerts inactive; the false `AuthorityFloatCritical` from the retired settlement account resolved on its own when the retired series disappeared.

### Ops tooling: rotation safety gates, admin-key file source, census tool

- `f3a54237d6` — `governance_parameter_change.py` funded-authority gate: `escrow_settlement_authority` and `bond_slash_authority` pay their own transaction fees after rotation, so sign-submit now refuses a target below the fee+headroom floor unless `--allow-unfunded-target` is passed deliberately (the failure mode seen when the settlement authority rotated to an address with no account row). `plan` prints each target's balance. `bridge_release_authority` (in-state credits, no account fees) and `escrow_fee_recipient` (receives only) are not gated.
- `49d8cc159e` — `register_bridge_validators.py` gains `BRIDGE_ADMIN_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE` — a path to a file holding the admin key (the workstation-custody `keys/bridge_admin.key` created for the dedicated bridge-admin address). A file key is single-source, so it is literal-class: `BRIDGE_ADMIN_ADDRESS` stays optional but is enforced when declared; `*_SOURCE` indirection still requires the binding declaration. `BRIDGE_ADMIN_ADDRESSES` service-side is untouched.
- `220d8f303a` — `scripts/ops/key_residue_census.py`: read-only scanner for the KR-7 residue census — walks files/units/scripts, reports file/line/variable plus *which retired address* matched (never the value), and derives the public address of every 64-hex key literal or `*_PRIVATE_KEY` value to report only the derived address and whether it is retired. Refuses out-of-root symlinks, skips binary files, reports file modes, exits non-zero on a retired hit, writes nothing.
- `9b9c4577c4` (unpushed) — `scripts/ops/watch_drift_check.py`: compares every bound `AITBC_WATCH_ACCOUNTS` extras entry with the address derived from its env-held key (`governance_proposer` ← `GOVERNANCE_PROPOSER_PRIVATE_KEY` in `aitbc-governance.env`, `validator_hub` ← `PROPOSER_KEY` in `validator-secrets.env`), prints OK|MISMATCH|ERROR|SKIP per role, exits 1 on drift. The follow-on check for the extras entries above: a rotated key that forgets to move its watch entry is caught instead of going blind.

