### MSG-8

- docs/design/msg8-direct-messages.md — Phase 0 design for encrypted
  direct messages: separate published X25519 key bound by a signed
  dm_key envelope, libsodium crypto_box with ephemeral sender keys,
  ciphertext-only index, client-side decryption, ~3900 B plaintext cap
  under the 4 KiB body cap. No forward secrecy; Phase 1 requires
  operator authorization plus independent crypto review.
### MSG-2e — lock scope rule (b): sealed-while-locked rows stay flagged

- ``after_lock`` is now interval-based: a row sealed while its topic (or
  an ancestor thread) was locked stays flagged after the unlock — an
  unlock clears only rows sealed after it. ``_ModState`` gains
  ``topic_intervals``/``thread_intervals`` ([start, end|None]); a second
  ``lock`` while open does not move the start, an ``unlock`` with no
  open interval is a no-op, and ineffective actions create no interval.
  "Locked now" routes (``locked``/``locked_by``, ``topic_lock``) are
  unchanged; LOCK_WALK_LIMIT still bounds descendants walked per root
  across all of that root's intervals.
- Design note: the "rows appear again after unlock" sentence replaced
  with rule (b); *confirm intended* marker removed.
- Tests: ``TestLockIntervals`` (8) in the production hash layout —
  during-lock permanence, two cycles, second-lock no-move, unlock-noop,
  non-moderator no-interval, thread intervals, same-block reorder,
  reorg re-opens the interval.


### MSG-2e (fix 2) — nested thread locks accumulate intervals

- ``_walk_locked_subtree`` stamped ``out[child] = intervals``, so a
  descendant reachable from several locked roots kept only the last
  root's intervals — a reply under an unlocked B dropped the still-open
  interval of ancestor A and read ``after_lock: false``. Descendants now
  accumulate (``setdefault().extend()``) with per-child copies, so no
  shared mutable lists can corrupt the read cache. LOCK_WALK_LIMIT stays
  a per-root walk bound; a shared descendant counts toward each root's
  budget and carries every reaching root's intervals.
- Tests: three more in ``TestLockIntervals`` — nested A-locked/B-unlocked
  keeping C flagged, the same with B locked first (dict-order
  independence), and the per-root walk bound with a shared descendant.

