### ETH-18: a young pending release logs below ERROR, not 'needs review' (2026-10-14)

During ETH-16's live withdrawal the hub withdraw monitor broadcast the
release and, 30 s later, logged ERROR "still unverifiable — needs review"
while the row stayed `releasing`; the next poll confirmed the receipt and
completed it. The tx had simply been mined below
`BRIDGE_RELEASE_CONFIRMATIONS` depth — a normal delay, not an incident.

`_release_nonce_outcome` now splits the nonce view into `nonce_consumed`
(the nonce was taken without a qualifying receipt — a real anomaly that
keeps ERROR, per ETH-15's documented intent) and `nonce_pending` (the tx
sits in the pending pool — routine). In `_resume_unfinished_releases`, a
`chain_view`/`nonce_pending` "unknown" younger than
`ETH_RELEASE_ABSENCE_GRACE_SECONDS` logs at INFO with the reason and age;
`verify_error`, `nonce_consumed` and aged rows keep ERROR, and the ERROR
line now names the reason. The sell-escrow inflight check applies the same
split at WARNING (its unknown path does persist `needs_review`). Log level
and log content only — no outcome, status, refund or parking decision
moved. Watcher note: `AITBCJournalErrors` counts `[ERROR]` lines per unit,
so routine young-release delays no longer feed it; real anomalies do.

### ETH-JOIN: settlement join hardening — retryable order_funded refusals, order_refund ref_id split, flag-off 503 (2026-10-15)

Pre-enable review of the ETH-19 sell-lock ⇄ settlement join (report-only
pass, then this fix pass) found three places where a caller-visible or
transient condition could strand escrowed AIT or corrupt an unrelated row.
All three are fixed while the feature is still dark on every host.

- A non-terminal `order_funded` refusal (5xx, 401/403, the flag-off 503)
  no longer writes a `lock:<tx_hash>` monitor mark — the lock stays in the
  escrow scan and retries next poll, counted under
  `stats["http_refused"]` with a WARNING line per refusal. Previously the
  mark was written unconditionally (`outcome="http_500"`), so one
  transient refusal permanently skipped the lock: never funded, never
  refunded — `orders.backing_tx_hash` stays NULL, so the remainder lane
  never even sees it. 404/409 keep their terminal refund path; transport
  errors were already unmarked.
- `order_refund` `ref_id` convention: the order's own lock-remainder
  refund keeps `+order_id`; a refund for an inbound lock the order could
  not take keys on `−order_id`. A stranger's dust lock naming the order
  can no longer squat the UNIQUE(kind,ref_id) row the owner's remainder
  refund needs — the same collision also protected a second stray lock,
  which is still a logged duplicate. No schema or live-data migration:
  `ref_id` is a signed INTEGER and the CHECK on `kind` is untouched.
- `POST /api/internal/exchange-settlement` answers 503
  `{"error": "exchange settlement disabled"}` while the exchange-side flag
  is off — 404 was indistinguishable from "order not found" and mapped to
  a lock refund. The supported rollout order (exchange flag first, wallet
  flag second) is now in `docs/apps/exchange/exchange.md`; with both
  flags off nothing changes — the monitor does not post at all.

Adjacent hardening in the same diff: `_set_status_and_mirror` skips the
tape mirror for `kind='order_refund'` — refund rows have no trade row and
`ref_id` shares the order/trade integer space, so mirroring one could post
`trade_state` onto an unrelated trade (or 404-pointlessly every poll).

Commits: `0d77eec16` (wallet monitor + schema comment + tests),
`6ff2adfac` (exchange endpoint + flag-off test). `exchange.md` rollout
ordering lands in the accompanying docs commit.


### CLI-1: exchange-settlement CLI gaps — node list/set-payout, order fund, settlements view (2026-10-15)

The trading CLI covered orders and bridge basics but none of the
settlement surfaces that `EXCHANGE_SETTLEMENT_ENABLED` (still dark on
every host) introduces. Five additive pieces close the gap; flag-off
behaviour is byte-identical everywhere.

- `aitbc exchange node list` — the existing `GET /api/exchange/nodes`
  had no CLI counterpart. Read-only, no wallet or signature.
- `aitbc exchange node set-payout --eth-address` — signs the
  `exchange_set_payout` envelope against `POST /api/exchange/nodes/payout`,
  mirroring `node register` (account nonce from `my-orders`, island
  chain_id). The address is EIP-55-checksummed before signing because
  the exchange refuses anything else.
- `GET /api/exchange/my-orders` now attaches the `funding` block
  (escrow, `required_ait`, `payload`) to rows that are `open` AND
  `funded_state='unfunded'` — only while the settlement flag is on.
  The block previously existed only in the place-order response, so a
  node that lost it had no way to re-read the lock target. Shapes are
  extracted into a shared read-only `_funding_block` used by both the
  place path and my-orders; no schema change.
- `aitbc exchange order fund <id> [--print]` — resolves the order's
  funding block and sends the escrow-lock TRANSFER with payload
  `{"sell_quote": <id>}` through the normal transaction-signing path.
  `--print` emits the equivalent `aitbc transactions send` line and
  broadcasts nothing. Refuses BUY orders (ETH prepay is out of scope),
  funded/expired/cancelled rows, and flag-off exchanges. Implemented
  and tested against mocks only — the help text says so; a real AIT
  transfer is an operator-named move.
- `GET /v1/bridge/settlements` + `aitbc wallet bridge settlements` —
  the `exchange_settlements` ledger (trade / `order_refund` rows,
  `needs_review`, error text) had no HTTP surface; state/kind filters
  and bounded limit/offset pagination. The route is **admin-keyed**
  (`require_admin_api_key`, same stance as `sell/quote`): hub nginx
  exposes the whole `/v1/bridge` mount publicly and the rows carry
  diagnostic error text plus release nonces/hashes.

Also fixed: the `order` group help still claimed "unfunded,
settlement-free". MCP tool modules regenerated — `order fund` and
`node set-payout` land under `dry_run`+`confirm` guards, `node list`
is read-only. `docs/api/wallet-openapi.json` regenerated for the new
route. Out of scope: hub default exchange URL/nginx routing (separate
operator decision), BUY-side funding, ETH-JOIN review items 4-7.

Commits: `11135740c` (exchange my-orders funding block + tests),
`8605f6b21` (CLI commands, wallet route/db/tests, MCP regen, openapi).


### CLI-2: CLI/MCP tooling gaps — file-info, env-names, exchange verify, MCP optional-arg schema (2026-10-15)

Four tooling gaps reported from the ops/MCP sweep (TOPOLOGY
2026-10-15-cli-mcp-gaps.md): every one had been worked around with
ssh+python because the CLI either dumped too much or required too much.
All additions are read-only; the generic `run-script --interpreter/--arg`
extension is deferred to the gap log as a separate reviewed item — it
widens a remote-exec surface.

- `aitbc system file-info --path <f> [--field <name>]` — the safe form
  of `system file` (which dumps whole content, including a wallet
  JSON's private_key). Reports size, mtime, owner, mode, type and the
  JSON top-level key names; deliberately no sha256 — a hash of a short
  secret file is brute-forceable offline. `--field` prints one scalar
  top-level field under a hard, case-insensitive denylist (private_key,
  secret, password, token, mnemonic, seed, key, api_key, `*_key`, and
  the same names nested in the value); denied names and >200-char
  values are explicit refusals. The allowlist now resolves the real
  path — a symlink escaping the allowed prefixes is refused.
  `/var/lib/aitbc/wallets` and `/etc/aitbc/credentials` are covered;
  `/root/.aitbc/wallets` is not.
- `aitbc system env-names --service <unit> [--names K...]` — variable
  NAMES only from `systemctl show -p EnvironmentFiles` (drop-ins
  included, `-` optional prefix handled, `/etc/aitbc/<unit>.env`
  fallback kept); unparsed lines report file+line number, never text;
  `--names` reports set/unset. `env-set` behaviour unchanged.
- `aitbc exchange verify` — wraps `scripts/ops/exchange_verify.py`:
  `sys.executable` argv exec (no shell), `--no-chain` default unless an
  RPC is given, `--db`/`--bridge-db` must be absolute existing regular
  files without `..` or symlinks, exit code and FAIL/WARN lines pass
  through. The script is stdlib-only and opens sqlite `mode=ro`.
- Generated MCP wrappers annotated every optional CLI option `T | None`
  but emitted no `= None` default, so FastMCP marked them all required
  (the sweep passed explicit nulls to work around it). The generator
  now appends `= None`; all 76 groups regenerated. The three new
  commands land as read-only MCP tools (`FORCE_READ_ONLY_PATHS`).

Tests: a denied field is proven absent from stdout and logs; `system
file` dump behaviour unchanged; an omitted MCP optional never reaches
the command line nor the string "None"; verify argv/passthrough/path
refusals covered. Spec: tasks/batch-05-build/CLI-2 (director-approved
with five amendments, all folded in).

Commits: `4b214fdb2` (MCP schema fix + regen + None-skip test),
`77ebd3aaf` (file-info + env-names + tests + docs), `facb65ebc`
