### CLI-4: `exchange order fund` forwards the wallet password options (2026-10-15)

`order fund` declared `--password/--password-file/--password-stdin` but
passed none of them to `load_wallet_for_payment`, so an encrypted wallet
aborted "No wallet password available" before the funding block was read —
`--print` included (found live on node2 during ETH-19 Phase D1; the D1
lock went out via `transactions send` instead). The loader cannot return
an address without decrypting (`_load_wallet` decrypts eagerly and its
password resolution is unconditional), so the command now reads the
wallet's plaintext `address` field — or the daemon record — for both
paths, then resolves the password exactly once right before the broadcast
and reuses it for `_send_transaction_impl`: `--password-stdin` is
consumed one time, `--print` on an encrypted wallet needs no password at
all, and a failed send exits nonzero instead of silently skipping the
success line. Sender-must-be-order-owner, the funding-block aborts, the
`{"sell_quote": <id>}` payload and the lock amount are unchanged.

Tests (`TestOrderFundEncryptedWallet`) run a real `_save_wallet`-encrypted
fixture through real password resolution with only the broadcast mocked:
`--password-file` reaches the send, wrong and missing passwords fail
closed, `--print` works with empty stdin, and `--password-stdin` is read
exactly once. The existing fund tests move from the loader mock to a real
unencrypted wallet file.

Commits: `f8933199e2` (password forwarding + plaintext address read +
real encrypted-wallet tests).

### WAL-2: wallet create/restore under the WAL-1 secret standard (2026-10-15)

`wallet create` was the last place a human had to type a wallet secret
twice on a TTY — a headless agent could only create unencrypted
(`--no-encrypt`) wallets or pre-seed the keyring. A separate
`resolve_new_wallet_password()` in `utils/wallet_secret.py` reuses the
WAL-1 sources, precedence and password-file checks (0600, ownership,
size), but creation semantics differ from signing: every
non-interactive source is self-confirming and returned once, only the
TTY fallback runs the two-prompt confirm loop — and only that path
writes the keyring, so a file/stdin/env password is never copied into
another store. Empty values that signing tolerates (`--password ""`,
empty env var) are refused for a new wallet. `--no-encrypt` is
unchanged (skips resolution entirely) and now explicitly refuses a
combined `--password*` flag instead of silently ignoring it.

`wallet restore` previously copied the backup JSON verbatim and
resolved no password at all (the WAL-2 design note's claim that it
called `_get_wallet_password` was stale — corrected in the note). It
now gains `--password-file`/`--password-stdin` — deliberately no
`--password` argv option: a plaintext backup is then re-encrypted under
the resolved password after the blob is proven to round-trip to the
backup's stored `address`, written atomically at mode 0600 with no
plaintext intermediate. A password flag on an already-encrypted backup
aborts before any write ("`--password*` applies only to plaintext
backups"); with no flags both encryption states remain verbatim copies.

Audit note: `genesis init --password` still forwards the secret onto
the `unified_genesis.py` subprocess argv (deliberately left in WAL-1),
`genesis reset --password` exposes it on the CLI's own argv (lands in
env files, not subprocess argv), and `_get_wallet_password`'s private
getpass survives in `wallet/staking.py`'s re-encrypt fallbacks (dead in
practice — encrypted wallets always arrive with a resolved password).

Tests: `cli/tests/test_wal2_create_restore_password.py` — 31 cases over
file/stdin/env/keyring/TTY sources, per-source precedence and leak
checks, create's flag matrix, and all four restore branches; the
existing wallet + wallet_secret suites, ruff, mypy ratchet,
check-cli-docs, check-mcp-cli-drift and check-c901-ratchet all pass.

Commits: `5f6178d52` (resolver + create/restore + tests + MCP regen).

