### ETH-JOIN-2: settlement join-review gaps 4–7 + order_refund squat residual (2026-10-15)

Second pass on the ETH-JOIN report, closing the four low-severity items
left open by ETH-JOIN (gaps 1–3) plus the residual it documented against
the `order_refund` ref_id sign split. All monitor-side; no schema change
and no live deployment — the code ships on the next authorized pull.

- **Gap 4 — normalized lock-hash compares.** `orders.backing_tx_hash` vs
  observed `tx_hash` was an exact-string equality at two sites: the
  re-observe check in `_order_lock_outcome` and the sender lookup in
  `_lock_sender`. An RPC case/prefix respelling would have dropped a
  funded lock into the refund lane, and on the sender path made
  `_lock_sender` fall back to `user_address` — refunding a stranger's
  lock remainder to the order owner. `_norm_tx_hash` (lower, strip `0x`)
  now normalizes both sides at both sites.
- **Gap 5 — no-payout trade skips and self-heals.** A trade whose seller
  had no `seller_eth_address` (order snapshot NULL *and* node_registry
  NULL) threw inside the ETH-release lane, pinning the settlement row in
  `needs_review` forever. `_ensure_trade_rows` now skips the trade before
  a row exists — retried each poll, so a seller who registers a payout
  address later settles on the next pass. The skip counts as
  `no_payout_skipped` in the poll summary and logs ERROR at most once
  per trade per hour (DEBUG between) so it cannot flood the journal.
- **Gap 6 — unlinked trades get a terminal mark.** Trades with NULL
  `buy_order_id`/`sell_order_id` logged an ERROR every poll forever with
  no terminal state. They now take a `unlinked-trade:<id>` monitor mark
  (detail carries the NULL links, `outcome="no_order_links"`) — one
  ERROR line at discovery, skipped silently afterwards. Kept the
  monitor-mark path rather than stamping `failed`: `failed` has no
  incoming edge in either `_TRADE_STATE_FORWARD` map, and adding one
  would have changed ETH-JOIN's mirror semantics.
- **Gap 7 — hash-domain comment.** `orders.tx_hash`/`backing_tx_hash`
  are 0x-prefixed chain transaction hashes; `envelope_hash` is the
  0x-less keccak of the signed order envelope — the shared word "hash"
  now carries an explicit never-compared comment in
  `simple_exchange/db.py`. Comment only.
- **Squat residual — owner-priority lock-keyed fallback.** ETH-JOIN's
  `−order_id` inbound-refund row can still be squatted by a stranger's
  dust lock: the owner's later under-cover lock then collided on UNIQUE
  and landed as `duplicate` needing a manual refund. When the
  `−order_id` slot is held, an *owner*-sender lock (the same
  `_sender_owns_order` check used for crediting) now falls back to
  `_lock_refund_ref_id(tx_hash)` — `−(int(hash[:15],16) | 2**59)`,
  deterministic per lock so a re-scan dedupes, bounded below `−2**59`
  so it cannot collide with any `±order_id` or trade id. Stranger
  duplicates stay bounded at one inbound-refund row per order — the
  dust-spam fee drain stays closed. A UNIQUE collision on the derived
  id still returns `duplicate` for operator review rather than crashing.
  Reader audit: `ref_id` is exact-match everywhere (`get_exchange_settlement`
  callers pass `±order_id` only; the mirror/refund lanes never read
  `order_refund` ref_ids; `exchange_verify.py`, routes, CLI and MCP are
  display-only), so the third ref_id class is opaque to every consumer.
  The `exchange_settlements` schema comment now documents all three
  classes. A `lock_tx_hash`-keyed column remains the cleaner long-term
  cut — left as a proposal, not implemented (no schema change was in
  scope).

Tests: 8 new cases in `test_exchange_settlement_monitor.py` — hash
normalization at both sites, derived-ref_id range/idempotence over
multiple hashes, owner-under-cover refund surviving a stranger squat,
second stranger lock still `duplicate`, re-poll writing no second row,
unlinked-trade terminal mark, no-payout skip counting and self-healing,
poll-summary counter. All failed before the source change; the full
65-test monitor suite, wallet suite, exchange suite, ruff, mypy
ratchet and check-c901-ratchet pass.

Commits: `8f9ac9f06` (monitor + schema comment + tests),
`88f0b3812` (exchange hash-convention comment).

### ETH-19B: the internal settlement endpoint accepts its loopback caller (2026-10-15)

`FastAPIRequestAdapter.__init__` never called
`BaseHTTPRequestHandler.__init__` and never set `self.client_address`, so
`_raw_peer_ip()` read a missing attribute, returned `""`, and
`_peer_is_loopback()` failed closed — `POST
/api/internal/exchange-settlement` answered 403 to every caller,
including the wallet settlement monitor's `order_funded` post for the
sealed D1 lock (found live on hub 15 Oct; the monitor correctly left the
row unmarked and retried each poll). The existing tests missed it
because they assign `client_address` on a fake handler.

The adapter now sets `client_address` from the ASGI request's client
(`("", 0)` when absent so the gate still fails closed), and `server.py`
runs uvicorn with `proxy_headers=False`. Evidence for that choice:
uvicorn 0.49.0's `ProxyHeadersMiddleware` rewrites `scope["client"]`
from `X-Forwarded-For` whenever the immediate peer is trusted (default
`127.0.0.1`, widenable via `FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS`) — a spoofable value
for a wire-peer gate; and an in-app ASGI wrapper cannot recover the
pre-middleware peer because uvicorn applies its middleware outside the
loaded app. The endpoint's second gate (refuse any request carrying
`X-Forwarded-For`/`X-Real-IP`) already made the composition safe, but
the raw peer removes the dependency entirely. The app's only other
`request.client` consumer is the `_client_ip` rate limiter, which
prefers nginx's `X-Real-IP` under its own `EXCHANGE_TRUSTED_PROXIES`
list — identical results with the rewrite off, verified by audit.

New tests in `test_internal_settlement.py` drive the real
`_dispatch`/adapter path through `TestClient(client=(host, port))`:
loopback + key + valid `prepaid_credit` → 200 with the deposit row
applied; loopback without key → 401; `10.1.2.3` → 403;
`::ffff:127.0.0.1` → 200 (IPv4-mapped loopback); loopback carrying
`X-Forwarded-For` or `X-Real-IP` → 403; a scope with no client → 403;
flag off → 503; and TestClient's `("testclient", 50000)` default is
documented as refused. A per-test fixture refreshes
`main._HANDLER_POOL` — `test_http_contract`'s real-uvicorn fixture shuts
it down on teardown, so the new tests are suite-order-proof.

Commits: `106987e87` (adapter client_address + proxy_headers=False +
real-adapter gate tests). Deploy/restart of the hub exchange is a
separate gated step — until then the running service still 403s.


### WAL-3: the last three non-standard secret paths join WAL-1 (2026-10-15)

Three leftovers closed out the shared wallet-secret standard.

`wallet bridge sell quote` and `sell lock` load the node wallet through
`_resolve_wallet` but could not take `--password-file`/`--password-stdin`,
leaving headless agents with only env vars and the keyring. Both commands
now carry `@wallet_password_options` and forward the trio into
`_load_wallet`'s lazy resolver — the secret resolves exactly once and an
unencrypted wallet still never asks. `sell status` never touches a wallet
file and is unchanged.

`wallet/staking.py`'s liquidity re-save paths still fell back to the
pre-WAL-1 `_get_wallet_password` (keyring, env, a private two-prompt TTY
loop) whenever the resolved password was falsy — dead code in practice,
since an encrypted wallet already failed closed in the resolver. The
fallback is gone and `_get_wallet_password` itself is deleted, removing
the last private `getpass` in `commands/`.

`genesis init --password` appended the secret to the
`unified_genesis.py` subprocess argv (visible in `ps` and shell history)
and `genesis reset` took `--password` as a bare option. Both commands now
expose the standard trio — `--password` deprecated with the usual
warning, plus `--password-file`/`--password-stdin` — and resolve through
`resolve_new_wallet_password` with the TTY prompt off. `init` hands the
value to the child by `AITBC_GENESIS_PASSWORD` in its environment
(child-only, zero filesystem lifetime — the script already writes
`.genesis_password` 0600, so env adds nothing weaker); the script reads
the env var when no `--password` argv is given, keeping direct
invocation working. `init` refuses a `--password*` flag without
`--create-wallet` — an ignored credential flag is the wrong failure
mode. `reset` keeps auto-generating when no flag is given.

Tests: `cli/tests/test_wal3_secret_leftovers.py` — 18 cases over real
encrypted wallets (`_save_wallet`, real resolvers): file/stdin flag
signing and broadcast for `sell lock`, wrong-password and no-password
fail-closed, stdin read exactly once, secret absence from output and
logs, `sell quote` unlock/fail-closed, the staking re-save round-trip
plus headless fail-closed before submit, genesis init's env-not-argv
boundary on all three sources, reset's resolved/None hand-off, and the
script-side `AITBC_GENESIS_PASSWORD` consumption with argv precedence.

Commits: `2d265c090` (sell trio + staking resolver + genesis env
hand-off + MCP regen + tests).

### ETH-JOIN-3: order_refund dust-lock bound + ref_id guard (2026-10-15)

Closes the two residuals ETH-JOIN-2's report named against the
owner-lock refund fallback — both are monitor-side in
`exchange_settlement_monitor.py`, no schema change, and neither alters
the ETH-JOIN/ETH-JOIN-2 semantics (http_* refusals stay unmarked,
sign-split + lock-keyed ref_ids, flag-off 503, the one-inbound-refund
bound per stranger per order).

- **Sub-fee inbound locks create no row.** `_lock_refund` — the single
  funnel for `remainder=False` refunds — now skips when
  `lock_ait <= _sweep_fee_ait()`, the same caller-side bound
  `_refund_stranded_locks` applies to the lock remainder. A lock worth
  less than its refund sweep stays in escrow instead of draining the
  shared float one sweep fee at a time. The check precedes both inserts
  in `_ensure_order_refund`, so a sub-fee owner lock gets neither the
  `−order_id` row nor the lock-keyed fallback. Outcome `below_fee`
  returns to `_scan_order_locks`, which deliberately writes no
  `lock:<hash>` mark — the lock is re-evaluated each poll so a fee
  change picks it back up, and the notice is rate-limited to INFO once
  per lock per hour (DEBUG between) behind a bounded, attacker-keyed
  map. Order 23's 1 AIT lock is far above the bound; its funded-path
  behaviour is unchanged.
- **`bad_tx_hash` guard on the fallback.** An empty or non-hex lock hash
  on the owner-fallback attempt no longer raises out of
  `_lock_refund_ref_id` — `_ensure_order_refund` returns `bad_tx_hash`,
  which the scan marks exactly like `duplicate` (terminal, no retry).
  The ERROR line names the hash problem and never claims a ref_id
  collision. The normal `−order_id` insert path does not read the hash
  and needs no guard.

Residual left open (stated, not implemented): an owner can still send
many *above-fee* locks against their own order — each fallback refund
costs the shared float one sweep fee at cost parity with the owner's
lock fee. Bounding that fully takes either the `lock_tx_hash` column
proposal (one refund row per lock, schema change) or a per-order cap on
fallback rows; `lock_tx_hash` is the cleaner cut.

Tests: 10 new items in `test_exchange_settlement_monitor.py` — sub-fee
stranger/owner locks write no row and no mark, just-above-fee refunds
for both sender classes, the bound following a monkeypatched
`_sweep_fee_ait`, empty/non-hex/None hash → `bad_tx_hash` through the
direct path and the scanner mark, and the hourly log rate limit. The
below-fee and bad-hash cases failed on the pre-change source; the full
75-test file passes.

### TEST-1: hook interpreter resolution + test-env isolation (2026-10-15)

Six 15-Oct gap-log rows, all repo tooling or test infrastructure — no
service runtime behaviour changes.

- **Shared repo-python resolver (gap rows 27/52).**
  `scripts/ci/repo-python.sh` is the single resolver for hook/CI
  scripts: `$PYTHON` env (validated — must exist and run a trivial
  import — then trusted), `<checkout>/venv/bin/python`, then the main
  checkout's venv located through `git rev-parse --git-common-dir`, so a
